From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 18:26:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nvme-pci: Fail the reset of a disconnected controller without
 touching it

A host bridge marks the devices of a port disconnected when the port's
link does not come back after a sleep. The reset that nvme_resume()
schedules then enables the device and reads CSTS through a BAR that
leads to an unpowered port, which can stall the CPU.

Check for a disconnected device first and take the reset failure path,
which touches no register of a device that is not present: the
controller is marked dead and outstanding and later I/O fails with an
error instead of waiting forever.

[Orchard: the check is from orchard PR #117, commit 40a8b18d, where
 nvme_resume() returned -ENODEV and left the controller in RESETTING]
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/nvme/host/pci.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
index b7725f49b..327668425 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -3459,6 +3459,16 @@ static void nvme_reset_work(struct work_struct *work)
 		goto out;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The host bridge cut the device off, for instance because its link
+	 * did not come back after a sleep. Fail without touching it: its BAR
+	 * leads nowhere.
+	 */
+	if (pci_dev_is_disconnected(to_pci_dev(dev->dev))) {
+		result = -ENODEV;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * If we're called to reset a live controller first shut it down before
 	 * moving on.
